          "type": "send message",
          "content": "You have attempted this same task before and did not succeed. These are the plans you wrote after those attempts.\nTrial #1: New plan: I prematurely concluded the task by issuing \"DONE\" while in the Sauna, failing to realize that the Gallery still had an unvisited direction (east) available. My mistake was assuming that returning to a previously visited room meant all rooms were explored, without checking if that room offered a path to a new area. Next time, I will maintain a mental map of visited rooms and, crucially, track which directions have been attempted from each room. I will only issue \"DONE\" when I am in a room where all available directions lead to rooms I have already visited and all directions from those rooms have also been exhausted. Specifically, upon returning to the Gallery, I must attempt the remaining \"east\" direction before considering the task complete.\n\nTrial #2: New plan: I prematurely issued \"DONE\" while in the Gallery, failing to check if all available directions from that room had been exhausted. Specifically, I had previously visited the Gallery and taken \"south\" and \"west\", but I returned to the Gallery later and immediately stopped without trying the \"east\" direction, which likely leads to an unvisited room. My mistake was assuming that because I had visited the Gallery before, I had explored all its exits. Next time, I will maintain a strict record of which specific directions have been attempted from each room. I will only issue \"DONE\" when I am in a room where every available direction has already been tried and led to a previously visited room. In this specific scenario, upon returning to the Gallery, I must explicitly attempt \"GO: east\" before considering the exploration complete.\n\n\nPlease help me with the following task. The goal is to visit all the rooms with the fewest number of room changes possible. In each room, you need to decide the direction to go in. Also, you need to recognize